The {{Nihongo|'''Imperial Household Agency'''|宮内庁|Kunai-chō}} (IHA) is a government agency of [[Okaiken]] responsible for state matters concerning the [[Imperial House of Okaiken]]. It manages the daily lives, security, and health of the Imperial Family, as well as the preservation of the [[Privy Seal of Okaiken]]. Historically one of the oldest institutions in the nation, the Agency was significantly reformed following the [[Referendums of 2011]] and the subsequent [[Jutsu Restoration]], which transitioned the monarchy from a dormant state back into an active role as the state authority of matters relating to the Imperial Family of Okaiken. 

== Organization and functions ==
The Agency is headed by the Grand Steward, a position currently held by [[Buichiro Kuroda]]. Unlike standard ministries, the IHA reports directly to the Sovereign through the [[Cabinet Office (Okaiken)|Cabinet Office]], maintaining a degree of institutional autonomy reflective of the Emperor's reserve powers.

Main organizational divisions include:
* The Grand Steward's Secretariat: Handles general administration and inter-governmental coordination.
* The Board of Chamberlains: Manages the personal schedules and daily welfare of Emperor Masahito and Empress Michiko.
* The Crown Prince's Household: Specifically handles the affairs of [[Crown Prince Tomohito]], including his industrial patronage and scientific research logistics.
* The Board of Ceremonies: Manages traditional rites, including [[Jōdo-shū]] observances and state functions.
* Archives and Mausolea Department: Maintains the historical records of the Honda dynasty and the imperial tombs.

== History ==
=== Pre-Republican Era (701–1968) ===
The Agency traces its lineage to the ancient {{Nihongo|Imperial Household Ministry|宮内省|Kunai-shō}} established by the ''Taihō'' Code. For over a millennium, it served as the administrative spine of the monarchy. Following the Meiji-style reforms of the late 19th century, it was elevated to a full Ministry, overseeing the vast crown estates and the nation's rapid industrialization during the Saito era.

=== The Republican Period (1968–2011) ===
In 1968, following a period of intense social upheaval, Okaiken transitioned to a republican form of government. The Imperial Household Ministry was abolished, and its functions were absorbed into the Ministry of Culture and Heritage. During these "Silent Years," the Honda family lived as private citizens (the [[Honda Family during the Republican Era|Honda-ke]]), though the department continued to maintain the imperial palaces and mausolea as national historical sites.

=== Jutsu Restoration (2011–present) ===
The [[Referendums of 2011]] resulted in the restoration of the monarchy and the recreation of the Imperial Household Agency as a modernized administrative body. Under the "Jutsu" era protocols, the Agency was tasked with reintegrating the Imperial Family into the nation's industrial and scientific sectors. This led to the creation of specialized sub-departments focused on royal scientific patronage, such as the logistics support for Tomohito's chemistry research and the [[Komorebi Protocol]] under Princess Kiko.

== Criticism and Public Perception ==
The Agency has faced scrutiny from both traditionalists and progressives. Conservative critics often argue that the IHA’s focus on "Functional Royalty" and industrial efficiency strips the throne of its ancient, spiritual mystique. Conversely, some academics have criticized the Agency for its strict control over the [[Kofun Period]] tombs, claiming that the IHA prioritizes the preservation of imperial lineage myths over archaeological transparency regarding Okaiken’s early continental ties.

